course: dictionary
lesson: 9109
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. The word smasher is a good example. In old British books and family talk, «She's a smasher» is warm praise for someone who is wonderful or very nice.
explain: The other meaning is plain and literal: a smasher is a thing that smashes or breaks things, like a can smasher. «Some mantis shrimps are smashers with clubs for hard shells» uses this meaning, because the shrimps break open hard shells.
explain: How do you know which meaning to use? Read the words around it. Is someone being praised, or is something being broken? Then swap your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
fact: Some words have more than one meaning.
fact: Context tells you which meaning the writer wants.
fact: «Smasher» can mean a person or thing that is wonderful, very nice or attractive. This is informal, old-fashioned British speech.
fact: «Smasher» can also mean a person or thing that smashes or breaks things, such as a can smasher.
fact: «Some mantis shrimps are smashers with clubs for hard shells» shows the breaking meaning: the shrimps use their clubs to smash shells.
exercise: «She's a smasher, that one.» What does smasher mean here? | *A person who is wonderful and very nice | A machine that crushes cans | A hard shell on the sand | A loud noise in the street
exercise: «Some mantis shrimps are smashers with clubs for hard shells.» What does smashers mean in this sentence? | Toys made of sand | *Creatures that break hard shells with their clubs | Kind, friendly people | Fish that stab soft fish from burrows
exercise: Grandma says, «You're a little smasher!» What is she telling you? | She thinks you are breaking things | She wants you to go away | *She thinks you are wonderful | She is cross about the noise
test: In a can smasher, what does the word smasher mean? | *Something that breaks or crushes things | A nice person | A song on the radio | A type of hard shell
test: Which sentence uses smasher to mean wonderful? | «The can smasher crushed twenty tins.» | «The sand was full of burrows.» | *«Your cake was a smasher, Grandma!» | «The mantis shrimp had clubs for hard shells.»
exam: Maya meets «record smasher» in a sports report. What is the best way to work out its meaning? | Guess that it means a nice person | *Read the words around it and check whether it fits the idea of breaking records | Skip the sentence and never come back to it | Decide it must be a kind of shrimp
